# MASTER EXECUTION DIRECTIVE

You are responsible for completing LORIN as a senior software architect, full-stack engineer, AI infrastructure engineer, cybersecurity engineer, cloud architect, database engineer, desktop/mobile developer, 3D real-time engineer, DevOps engineer and quality assurance lead.

Your responsibility is to transform the existing LORIN application into a complete, secure, intelligent, persistent, proactive, cross-platform personal AI assistant.

This is an existing-project upgrade.

**It is not permission to create a replacement application, a new starter template, a disconnected demonstration, a collection of mock interfaces or a smaller application that merely resembles LORIN.**

Work directly on the latest complete LORIN source.

The result must preserve existing functionality while implementing and integrating all requirements in this specification.

## 0.1 Absolute development rules

1. Inspect the entire source before modifying it.
2. Establish the actual current source baseline and verify its identity.
3. Preserve the existing application architecture, data models, working routes, services, assets, project history and functionality unless a documented technical reason justifies a migration.
4. Never replace working code simply because a different implementation is easier.
5. Do not create duplicate versions of existing services.
6. Reuse and improve existing Primary Node, Device Centre, model gateway, Knowledge Vault, Memory, task and security implementations.
7. Preserve compatibility with existing user data.
8. Back up the source before major modifications.
9. Use version control, controlled checkpoints and documented migrations.
10. Maintain a complete changed-file manifest.
11. Keep the project runnable after every development stage.
12. Implement backend functionality rather than merely creating interface controls.
13. Never claim that a feature works simply because its screen exists.
14. Never fabricate statistics, provider status, model availability, hardware specifications, synchronization progress, task results or telemetry.
15. Do not silently switch private data to public cloud services.
16. Do not allow an AI model to approve its own permissions or authorize its own actions.
17. Do not expose secrets, private documents, database dumps or personal credentials in source archives.
18. Do not introduce undocumented breaking changes.
19. Never remove an existing feature without documenting the reason and migration.
20. Continue implementation through actual verification and delivery, not just planning.

## 0.2 Zero-defect engineering standard

The objective is to deliver a stable, secure and thoroughly tested product.

Do not promise that an application is error-free merely because tests pass.

Instead:

- Detect errors.
- Reproduce failures.
- Identify root causes.
- Correct defects.
- Add regression tests.
- Re-run relevant tests.
- Test interactions between modules.
- Test permission boundaries.
- Test failure and recovery scenarios.
- Record all unresolved issues.

No known critical or high-severity defect may be silently ignored.

If an environment limitation prevents verification, clearly identify it. Never change a test, disable a security control, fabricate a result or weaken validation merely to produce a passing build.

